	8. Deck 7: Mzansi Slang, SASL Gestures & Everyday Cultural Tropes
Nothing makes a South African party game louder than players trying to act out local idioms, traffic habits, and multi-lingual slang borrow-words from isiZulu, isiXhosa, Sesotho, Afrikaans, and English5. As highlighted by The TEFL Academy's coverage of the 2023 SASL Bill announcement, young Deaf South Africans have also popularized official South African Sign Language (SASL) slang gestures that work naturally as physical charades cues5.
To onboard new players in your app's tutorial screen, you can feature six signature Mzansi physical cues as visual instruction cards: (1) The Bacardi Dance (performing the rhythmic hip move from Tyla's "Water" challenge), (2) SASL "Sharp Sharp" & "Vibe" (flashing a rhythmic doubled-up thumbs-up for Sharp sharp, or a loose flick-of-the-wrist borrowed from Amapiano dance culture for Vibe), (3) The "Shoe Kick Transition" (balancing a sneaker on your foot in a karate-style side kick before jumping into a new outfit pose), (4) "Now, Just Now, Now Now" (pointing to a wristwatch with varying urgency to distinguish South Africa's three tiers of time), (5) Dricus "Stillknocks" du Plessis (pinching your nostrils for "8% oxygen" before strapping on a UFC belt), and (6) Durban Bunny Chow (miming scooping spicy curry out of a hollowed-out loaf of bread)5.
